<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Artois</h1><p class="st-meta">1237 CE – 1659 CE · capital Arras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern French county of cloth towns and tapestries, created as a royal appanage and later held by Burgundy and Spain before returning to France.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Artois came to the French crown in 1191 through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-hainaut">Isabella of Hainaut's</a> dowry. In 1237 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX</a> created it as a county for his brother Robert I, who was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-mansurah">Mansourah</a> in 1250 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. Robert's descendants ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> was a major cloth and banking town, and its tapestries became so famous that 'arras' meant a wall hanging in English and Italian. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-count-of-artois">Robert II</a> was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> in 1302, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahaut-countess-of-artois">Mahaut</a> defended her inheritance against her nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-iii-of-artois">Robert III</a>. In 1384 Artois passed with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1477 Artois stayed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> in 1640, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659 gave most of the county to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-artois.jpg" alt="County of Artois" width="320" height="194"><figcaption>Image: Patricia.fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Artois.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1237 CE – 1659 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert I of Artois, Robert II of Artois, Mahaut of Artois</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Artois</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=county-of-artois" aria-label="County of Artois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4744 430 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Artois lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5064 686L5054 728M5064 686L5118 774M5064 686L5102 664M5064 686L5099 670"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5118 774h0M5102 664h0M5099 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5064 686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5064 686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arras. <a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=county-of-artois">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

